# apps/web and apps/api behind Caddy on one registrable domain (see
# docs/architecture.md's Deployment section, Front epic #1 / GH issue
# #15): required so the HttpOnly/SameSite=Lax session cookie is sent
# automatically on browser calls to either service, with no CORS
# configuration needed.
#
# The site address comes from `SITE_ADDRESS` (infra/.env, passed through by
# docker-compose.yml) — #141. A domain name there (`maison.example.org`)
# turns on Caddy's automatic HTTPS: a certificate is obtained and renewed
# for it (kept in the `caddy_data` volume), port 80 only redirects to 443,
# and every request, credentials included, travels over TLS. That needs
# the name to resolve to this host and ports 80 and 443 to reach it from
# the Internet. Unset, it falls back to `:80`, plain HTTP on any host name:
# the local stack of infra/.env.example, never a public deployment — the
# README's "Running it for real" has the manoeuvre.

# How long a client may take to send a request (#219). apps/web and
# apps/api bound every request body themselves — 30 s of silence, 1 kB/s
# on average after 10 s, 15 min in all, answered 408 with
# `Connection: close` (`manage_our_home_http_guard`). This is the backstop
# behind them, not the bound itself.
#
# - `read_body` is Go's `ReadTimeout`: one deadline for the whole request,
#   headers included, from the first byte — a total, not an inactivity
#   bound. At 16 min it sits one minute above the applications' 15, so a
#   slow body is answered by the application's 408. When Caddy's own
#   deadline goes first the answer is not one to rely on: measured on
#   v2.11.4 with it at 5 s and an upstream that waits for the whole body,
#   the client got a 504 on the first run, then an empty
#   `200 OK` with `Connection: close` on every run after it.
# - It does not reach the Messagerie WebSocket: once `reverse_proxy` has
#   switched a connection to WebSocket, the connection stays up past
#   `read_body` (measured the same way: a socket held 12 s kept both
#   directions working).
# - `read_header` 10 s: headers are small, and a client trickling them
#   holds a connection before any application has seen the request.
# - `idle` 5 min is Caddy's default, written next to the other two so the
#   three connection bounds read in one place.
{
	servers {
		timeouts {
			read_header 10s
			read_body 16m
			idle 5m
		}
	}
}

# An application's 408 must reach the client when it is sent, not when
# the client has finished sending. Go's server, answering a request whose
# body is not fully read and is under 256 KiB, first reads and discards
# the rest of it — unless the response says `Connection: close`. The
# application sets that header, but it is hop-by-hop, so `reverse_proxy`
# drops it on the way out; without putting it back, a body dripped at
# 1 byte/s through this proxy got no answer at all within 20 s, where the
# application alone answered 408 at 10 s (measured on v2.11.4).
(close_on_408) {
	@body_timeout status 408
	handle_response @body_timeout {
		header Connection close
		copy_response
	}
}

{$SITE_ADDRESS::80} {
	# Security headers (#141), on every response of both applications —
	# set here once rather than in each of them.
	#
	# Content-Security-Policy. Nothing the pages load comes from elsewhere
	# (stylesheet and fonts are served by apps/web under /assets, DESIGN.md),
	# so `default-src 'self'` covers images, fonts and `fetch`/WebSocket
	# (the Messagerie's socket is same-origin, under /api).
	# - `script-src` allows no file and no inline script at all, except the
	#   few apps/web still writes inline, each by the SHA-256 of its exact
	#   text; `'unsafe-hashes'` extends that to event-handler attributes
	#   (`onclick="…"`). Which string each hash stands for, and the test that
	#   fails when one changes without the other, are in
	#   apps/web/src/csp.rs — run `cargo test -p manage_our_home_web csp`
	#   after touching any inline script: its failure prints the list to
	#   paste here.
	# - `worker-src 'self'`: the service worker of the reminder
	#   notifications (#306), `/sw.js`, served by apps/web. Without it the
	#   browser falls back on `script-src`, which allows no file, and
	#   refuses to register it.
	# - `style-src 'self'` keeps `<style>` elements out; `style-src-attr
	#   'unsafe-inline'` lets the handful of `style="…"` attributes in the
	#   routes through (an avatar's colour, a column's alignment). A browser
	#   that predates `style-src-attr` falls back on `style-src` and drops
	#   those attributes: cosmetic, nothing stops working.
	# - `frame-ancestors 'none'` (and `X-Frame-Options DENY` for browsers
	#   older than it): no page of the application can be framed.
	# - `form-action 'self'`, `base-uri 'none'`, `object-src 'none'`: an
	#   injected `<form>`, `<base>` or plugin cannot send anything elsewhere.
	#
	# Strict-Transport-Security: a browser that once reached the site over
	# HTTPS refuses plain HTTP to it for a year. Browsers ignore it on a
	# plain-HTTP response, so the `:80` local stack is unaffected. No
	# `includeSubDomains`: the domain's other hosts are not this file's.
	#
	# Referrer-Policy no-referrer: no URL of the application ever leaves in a
	# `Referer` — `/verify-email?token=…` carries its token in the query
	# string.
	header {
		Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'unsafe-hashes' 'sha256-3y+tCmWcFp9x2K4uMZvV/ZEud2bfDnOltGdCBSw3YkI=' 'sha256-dGJiRXhRBoKzoa7jUS7f1mjmtnyDDbXHuZ7qw0wIL1w=' 'sha256-osjxnKEPL/pQJbFk1dKsF7PYFmTyMWGmVSiL9inhxJY=' 'sha256-6K7iZlEt9YCNTQ56Gu2hbVcZJoAdQ55xrHvDQ2o6dFU=' 'sha256-lYsQxrqqhY6skx46GFkuY7qu8trJVbJOMW/RoP6fdZs=' 'sha256-VrtvwbP0BSW5sTTEz0v9sve897Z4TyyoPUrKKRQxcM8=' 'sha256-B22SHpa71PIueUIDWDfK878EgjoNVDy8Sge3DFo1X3I=' 'sha256-bI+eIkmZUe+KpE09YEvnyjC4sQyD69AeTeeFkPEf0pk=' 'sha256-9DG8InsKFhCjrQuK1k5n90WvpWjoJ9VNUAhA1xPn1TY='; worker-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy no-referrer
	}

	# apps/web inlined its stylesheet into every response until #89, and
	# `encode` is what made that hold: the same ~27 kB of CSS travelled with
	# every page and uncompressed it alone overflowed the ~14 kB first
	# congestion window the inlining existed to fit inside. The sheet is now
	# its own response (`/assets/style-<digest>.css`, DESIGN.md → Livraison
	# du CSS), and `encode` still carries it — 27 213 bytes of text/css down
	# to 10 398 gzipped, with the `Cache-Control: immutable` passed through
	# untouched. It covers the HTML documents and apps/api's JSON too.
	#
	# `zstd gzip`, in that order of preference, is the whole menu Caddy v2
	# can produce on the fly: it does not compress to brotli at runtime
	# (only serves pre-compressed .br files), so there is no third entry to
	# add. Responses under `minimum_length` (512 bytes by default) are left
	# alone, which is the right call for the small API payloads.
	#
	# zstd first even though it measured 2.6 % *larger* than this same
	# Caddy's gzip on every main route (its encoder is tuned for speed):
	# 200 bytes on a 8 kB page, against a decoder that is several times
	# faster on the phone doing the decoding. Both fit the budget.
	#
	# BREACH: the only secret this application ever puts in a compressible
	# body is the password-reset token, reflected into a hidden input by
	# apps/web/src/routes/auth/reset_password.rs when it re-renders the form
	# after a refused POST (since #142 the landing GET carries no token: it
	# rides in the link's fragment, read client-side). The attack needs a second,
	# attacker-chosen string in that same response to compress against; that
	# page has none (its one variable is the token, and it must parse as a
	# UUID), and the token is single-use and expires after 24 h
	# (apps/api/src/auth/mod.rs: `consumed_at` is checked and set in one
	# `FOR UPDATE` transaction, `PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_HOURS`). So no route is
	# excluded from `encode` — see the PR for #83 for the full reasoning.
	encode zstd gzip

	# The two upload routes (#219), refused at this hop once the body passes
	# what apps/api's route accepts: `storage::MAX_UPLOAD_BODY_BYTES`,
	# 20 MiB of attachment plus 64 KiB of multipart framing = 21 037 056
	# bytes. A body over it is answered 413 here instead of travelling to an
	# application that would refuse it. Every other route keeps the
	# applications' own 2 MiB default. `*` stands for one path segment.
	@upload {
		method POST
		path /agenda/*/attachments /api/groups/*/events/*/attachments
	}
	request_body @upload {
		max_size 21037056
	}

	# Any answer to a request that carries a body must reach the client when
	# it is sent, whatever its status (#244). It is the same Go rule as
	# `close_on_408` above, but not only for the 408: an application may
	# answer before reading the body at all — 401 without a session, 403,
	# 503 from the upload gate, apps/web's 303 to /login — and on a body
	# under 256 KiB, Go's server then reads and discards the rest before the
	# response goes out. Caddy's own 502, when an application is down, is
	# held the same way. Measured on v2.11.4 over HTTP/1.1, against an
	# upstream that answers without reading, with a raw-socket client
	# sending 100 kB at 2 kB/s: 401, 403, 503, 303 and the 502 got no
	# answer within 20 s, where the 408 (the only one `close_on_408`
	# matched) came after the first 100 bytes, within 0.06 s; with this
	# rule all of them do.
	#
	# Keyed on the request, not the status: which responses come before the
	# body is read is the applications' business, not something to list
	# here. The cost is one TCP connection per HTTP/1.1 request with a
	# body, where keep-alive would have reused it; GET, HEAD and OPTIONS
	# keep it. A GET that does carry a body is not matched — only its own
	# sender waits.
	#
	# HTTP/1.1 only. HTTP/2 does not hold these answers (same upstream, the
	# 401 came back long before the body was all sent), and there Go turns
	# `Connection: close` into a GOAWAY: every POST would end the one
	# multiplexed connection the page's other requests share (measured:
	# without `protocol http/1.1`, curl got a GOAWAY after each POST over
	# h2 and reconnected; with it, it reuses the connection).
	#
	# Set before `reverse_proxy` runs, so its removal of the upstream's
	# hop-by-hop headers does not reach it.
	@has_body {
		not method GET HEAD OPTIONS
		protocol http/1.1
	}
	header @has_body Connection close

	# `X-Forwarded-For` (#178): nothing to configure here, but the behaviour
	# is load-bearing, so it is written down. Measured against `caddy:2`
	# (v2.11.4) with this same `handle` layout, on both paths:
	#
	#   curl -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 8.8.8.8' http://caddy/login
	#     -> upstream sees X-Forwarded-For: <the caller's real address>
	#
	# `reverse_proxy` sets the header to the address it accepted the
	# connection from, and a value the caller supplied is **discarded**, not
	# appended to — there is no `trusted_proxies` here, so no client counts
	# as a proxy. So the header apps/web and apps/api receive from this hop
	# cannot be chosen by the browser.
	#
	# apps/api does not take that on faith (`src/client_ip.rs`): it honours
	# the header only from a peer inside TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS and reads the
	# rightmost entry that is not itself such a peer. If this proxy is ever
	# put behind another one, the chain keeps working and this comment stops
	# being the whole story — the entry to trust would then be the one that
	# outer proxy appended.
	handle /api/* {
		uri strip_prefix /api
		reverse_proxy api:8080 {
			import close_on_408
		}
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy web:3000 {
			import close_on_408
		}
	}
}
